<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aosta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augusta Praetoria</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aosta is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. It was founded in 25 BCE as the Roman colony of Augusta Praetoria Salassorum after the conquest of the Salassi, and preserves its Roman street grid, walls, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and the Arch of Augustus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anselm-of-canterbury">Anselm of Canterbury</a> was born there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aosta.jpg" alt="Aosta" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tiia Monto · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aosta_and_mountains.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Aosta is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, 110 km north-northwest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, at the confluence of the Buthier and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dora-baltea">Dora Baltea</a>. It stands at the junction of the Great and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-st-bernard-pass">Little St Bernard Pass</a> routes, and near the Italian entrance of the Mont Blanc Tunnel and the Great St Bernard Tunnel. This position gave the city considerable military importance in antiquity. Its location in the rain shadow of the Mont Blanc massif leaves it with cool to very cold winters, hot summers and relatively dry conditions throughout the year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Aosta was settled in proto-historic times and became a centre of the Salassi, many of whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> killed or sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 25 BCE. The campaign was led by Terentius Varro, who founded the Roman colony of Augusta Praetoria Salassorum, housing 3,000 retired veterans. After 11 BCE Aosta became the capital of the province of Alpes Graies. Its layout was that of a Roman military camp.</p><p>After the fall of the Western Empire the city was conquered in turn by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, who had annexed it to their Italian kingdom, were expelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Frankish Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-the-short">Pepin the Short</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> it gained importance as a post on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-francigena">Via Francigena</a>, which led from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>In the 10th century Aosta became part of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and after its fall in 1032 it passed to the lands of Count Humbert I of Savoy. The inhabitants were granted the privilege of holding an assembly of the states-general in 1189, and an executive council was nominated from it in 1536, which lasted until 1802. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> restored Savoy rule, the council was reconstituted and recognized by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-albert-of-sardinia">Charles Albert of Sardinia</a>, at the birth of his grandson Prince Amedeo, who was created duke of Aosta.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Aosta is the capital of the bilingual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aosta-valley">Aosta Valley</a>. The Roman town walls are preserved almost in their entirety, enclosing a rectangle of 724 by 572 metres, with twenty towers in all. The Porta Praetoria, the eastern gate dating to the 1st century, keeps its original form apart from the marble covering. The Roman theatre, built in the late reign of Augustus, held up to 4,000 spectators and its southern façade, 22 metres tall, remains. Outside the walls stands the Arch of Augustus, built to celebrate the victory over the Salassi. Anselm of Aosta, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> from 1093 to 1109, was born in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aosta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 25 BCE Aulus Terentius Varro Murena conquered them for Augustus, selling some 36,000 into slavery, and founded <b>Augusta Praetoria</b>, modern Aosta, on their land.</blockquote><a href="/state/salassi">Salassi</a></li></ul></section>
